  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increased quality and labour market relevance with skills development / TVET provision</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ILO has supported Montenegro in developing a skills system that is aligned with EU standards in Technical Vocational Education and Training (TVET) and Lifelong Learning (LLL). While progress has been made through initiatives such as dual TVET, recognition of prior learning, and the establishment of sectoral skills councils, challenges remain in adapting to digitalization, addressing labour market needs, and ensuring financial sustainability. 

One key challenge is enhancing the digital skills of TVET teachers and students. Many teachers have not received adequate training in digital tools and e-learning, thereby negatively affecting students&#x2019; readiness for the modern labour market. This issue is particularly pronounced in Montenegro&#x2019;s northern regions, where access to digital resources remains scarce. The ILO supports capacity building in e-pedagogy, the development of core employability skills, and the creation of networks of TVET teachers for digitalization. 

Another priority is strengthening the recognition of prior learning (RPL). The Ministry of Education aims to improve the RPL system to enhance lifelong learning opportunities and better align skills with labour market demands. Ensuring the financial viability of dual TVET remains crucial. The COVID-19 crisis has significantly reduced employers&#x2019; ability to contribute financially, making it necessary to explore new funding mechanisms that sustain employer engagement. 

Montenegro also seeks to improve sectoral skills matching by strengthening sector-based skills forecasting to make TVET more responsive to labour market demands. As part of a joint UN programme, the ILO aligns its efforts with the National Strategy for Digitalization of Education (2022&#x2013;2027) to integrate digital tools into education, particularly benefiting vulnerable communities. These efforts contribute to the modernization of TVET, improving its responsiveness to labour market needs and enhancing the quality of training programmes.</narrative>
  </description>
